# `SELECT a, b` after a pipe is a plain column list, so `project` is certain.
mode: syntax
---- query
from t | SELECT a, b | take 1
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..30
  PIPELINE@0..29
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "t"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    PROJECT_OP@9..20
      IDENT@9..15 "SELECT"
      WHITESPACE@15..16 " "
      NAMED_EXPR@16..17
        NAME_REF@16..17
          IDENT@16..17 "a"
      COMMA@17..18 ","
      WHITESPACE@18..19 " "
      NAMED_EXPR@19..20
        NAME_REF@19..20
          IDENT@19..20 "b"
    WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
    PIPE@21..22 "|"
    WHITESPACE@22..23 " "
    TAKE_OP@23..29
      IDENT@23..27 "take"
      WHITESPACE@27..28 " "
      LITERAL@28..29
        LONG@28..29 "1"
  WHITESPACE@29..30 "\n"

error[DS0105]: SQL keyword `SELECT` is not an operator
  --> query:1:10
  |
1 | from t | SELECT a, b | take 1
  |          ^^^^^^ this is SQL
  |
  = help: choose or compute columns with `project`: `project a, b`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from t | project a, b | take 1
which parses without errors
